# Short-duration insurance and separate reinsurance

Status: internally checked PAA illustration, not a general IFRS 17 engine or
an actuarial estimate. MC19, MC22; gaps G7 and G16.

## Conditions that make the example work

Both the issued contracts and the proportionate reinsurance treaty have
one-year coverage. Underlying policies and treaty start together and are
recognised then. Both service patterns are uniform; there are no acquisition
cash flows, investment components or significant financing effects. Remaining
gross coverage is assumed non-onerous. Claim and recovery cash flows are
expected within one year of the underlying claims being incurred. Gross risk
adjustment 2 and transferred-risk adjustment 1 are separately supplied inputs;
zero expected reinsurer non-performance effect is another explicit assumption.

The manuscript now states separate treaty eligibility and recognition timing.
Short underlying policies alone would not establish that a longer or differently
timed reinsurance contract qualifies for the same simplification.

## Proposed accounting and exact reconciliation

Capital 20 plus received premium 120, less ceded premium 24 and claims paid 30,
plus recovery received 15 gives cash 101. Half of gross service releases 60
from remaining coverage into revenue; half of ceded service releases 12 from
the reinsurance coverage asset into expense. Gross incurred claims of 40 plus
risk adjustment 2 give expense 42 and, after payment 30, liability 12. Treaty
recoveries of 20 plus transferred-risk adjustment 1 give recovery income 21
and, after receipts 15, recovery asset 6.

| Reconciliation | Amount |
| --- | ---: |
| Assets: cash 101 + reinsurance coverage 12 + claims recovery 6 | 119 |
| Liabilities: gross remaining coverage 60 + incurred claims 12 | 72 |
| Profit: revenue 60 − gross claims 42 − ceded service 12 + recovery 21 | 27 |
| Equity: opening capital 20 + profit 27 | 47 |

The [journal report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) checks every operation's
balance and the final identity. Changing transferred risk to 1.5, holding other
inputs fixed, changes the recovery asset to 6.5 and profit to 27.5. An illustrative
expected non-performance reduction of 2 instead produces recovery asset 4 and
profit 25. Those are conditional calculation checks, not estimated risks. They
show why a blanket “halve all gross balances” operation is wrong for this model.
Gross and ceded portfolio balances remain separate; the coverage/claims split
is an internal roll-forward, not an assertion that each is a required face line.

## Inspected authority and strongest objections

[AASB 17](../../research/standards/aasb17.pdf): pp. 15–18, paragraphs 53–59,
60–70A, establish PAA eligibility/movement, short cash-flow discounting relief,
separate reinsurance recognition and eligibility, non-performance and transferred
risk. Paragraphs 78–82 (pp. 19–20) distinguish issued and held portfolios and
their performance. B126–B127 (p. 54) use passage of time unless expected risk
release differs significantly and require reconsideration when facts change.
Paragraphs 62–62A matter to when the treaty first enters the example; 69(b)
matters separately from 53(b).

This official Australian compilation applies to annual periods beginning
on/after 1 January 2023 and before 1 July 2026, with amendments through
15 December 2022 (PDF pp. 1, 5). It is inspected IASB-equivalent paragraph
support within the stated jurisdiction/version limits, not blanket current
IFRS compliance. [Source hashes](../../research/standards-support.json) bind
the version read.

The strongest objection is that one or more assumed eligibility, service,
cash-flow timing or risk inputs may fail for a real group. A multi-year treaty,
significant differences in risk release, onerous underlying contracts, treaty
limits/disputes or longer claim settlement changes the accounting work needed.
A chain-ladder point projection does not supply the risk adjustment or an
entire predictive law.

